   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the town law, in relation to authorizing the prevention or reduction of waste matter consisting of carbon components of energy waste from residential properties",
    "basePrintNo": "S6073",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6073-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §198, add §209-i, Town L",
    "lawSection": "Town Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6073\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the town law, in relation to authorizing the\nprevention or reduction of waste matter consisting of carbon\ncomponents of energy waste from residential properties\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThis legislation seeks to clarify the ability of towns to use refuse\nand garbage districts to deal with carbon waste by helping residential\nhomeowners to finance energy audits and energy efficiency improvements\nto prevent or reduce the generation of carbon-based waste in order to\npromote the health and welfare of New York citizens.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nAmends subdivision 9 of section 198 of the Town Law and adds a new\nsection 209-I to the Town Law in relation to expanding refuse and\ngarbage districts to include prevention or reduction of waste matter\nconsisting of carbon components of energy waste from residential\nproperties in either gaseous or solid form.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nResidential energy efficiency programs help decrease local and\nstatewide electricity and heating fuel demand and reduce harmful\nimpacts by reducing emissions and contributions to climate change. In\nthe Town of Babylon, which established a home energy efficiency\nprogram in 2008, testing showed that participating houses were made,\non average, nearly 30% more energy efficient. This reduces by 28% the\nC02 that would have been emitted without program investments. These\nresults constitute a public purpose of promoting health and welfare of\nresidents.\n\nThe provisions of this bill allow towns to include the operation of a\nhome energy efficiency program within their refuse and garbage\ndistricts (established pursuant to Town Law article 12 and Town Law\narticle 12-A (section 190).  Consequently, towns may contract for home\nenergy audits, invest in energy conservation or efficiency\nimprovements in homes and arrange with homeowners and contractors to\ninstall or implement them.\n\nFurthermore, the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009\nincluded monies for Energy Efficiency and Conservation Block Grants\n(EECBG) for which home energy efficiency programs would be eligible.\nThe addition of home energy efficiency programs to the purview of\ngarbage and refuse districts will provide a mechanism through which\nEECBG monies can be channeled to municipalities employing such\nprograms.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew 2009\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nThe costs associated with programs like the Town of Babylon's can be\nentirely reimbursed through payments made available from homeowners'\nenergy savings, including administrative costs.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
